%I A401617 #15 Nov 09 2026 22:32:07
%S A401617 1,1,1,2,2,2,2,3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,4,4,3,2,2,2,3,2,3,3,2,3,3,3,3,4,5,5,4,
%T A401617 3,3,2,2,2,3,4,3,3,3,2,2,3,4,4,3,2,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,5,6,6,5,4,4,
%U A401617 3,3,3,3,3,3,2,2,2,2,3,4,4,4,3,3,3,4,3
%N A401617 Maximum number of consecutive equal digits of n in any base >= 2.
%C A401617 Records high terms occur at all n which are powers of 2 minus 1 (A000225), as these comprise all digits of 1 in base 2.
%C A401617 n = 3 and n = 5 are the only cases for which n-1 is the only base that produces a number with consecutive equal digits (11_2 and 11_4 respectively). Proof: only numbers comprised of alternating 1s and 0s in base 2 (A000975) would qualify, but all such numbers equal all 1s or all 2s in base 4.
%C A401617 Conjecture: for each k >= 2, there are an infinite number of terms for which a(n) = k.
%H A401617 Charles L. Hohn, <a href="/A401617/b401617.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A401617 a(13) = 3 because 13 = 111_3, which contains 3 consecutive equal digits.
%e A401617 a(80) = 4 because 80 = 1010000_2 = 2222_3, each of which contain 4 consecutive equal digits.
%o A401617 (PARI) a(n)={if(!n, return(1)); my(m=0); for(b=2, max(2, n-1), my(d=digits(n, b), c=0); for(i=1, #d, if(i==1 || d[i]!=d[i-1], c=1, c++); m=max(m, c); if(#d<=m, break(2)))); m}
%Y A401617 Cf. A171901, A178905.
%K A401617 nonn,base,new
%O A401617 0,4
%A A401617 _Charles L. Hohn_, Oct 30 2026
